nning of the  pandemic i think there was a bit of fear  mongering about  how it had come we were all concerned  worried about you know how did it get  here  and because we realized that it had  transmuted from sort of the geological  system into the human system  there was this concern that maybe we  will again sort of start looking at  nature as the enemy but i  hope what will happen and is happening  not as much as i would like  but i hope that the right message is  that we have to live  in co-existence with nature that nature  is a huge thing  that nature is what nourishes us and if  we restrict the space of nature if we  restrict the space of biodiversity  then nature will hit back in the ways  for example that your guest was talking  about with dengue  or in the way that we have seen with  coronavirus that in some ways the big  lesson is  that there is no human health without  ecological health  that the two come together let's talk  about the management of climate change  managing the pandemic has required an  incredible amount of collective action  and sacrifice  which climate change will also require  based on what you've seen so far from  the pandemic  should we be optimistic or pessimistic  about the fight against climate change  at this point i'm neither but i do do  hope that we should be very reflective  about what has happened we should try to  think about okay what  what just happened what did we learn  about ourselves about our own behavior  and we should be very concerned in some  ways this pandemic gives you an early  warning if you will  of what a truly or a reminder because  we've had this before what a truly  global  crisis looks like you know we've talked  about climate as  as as a crisis possibly in the future  and having this in your face  makes you think and should make you  think there are good lessons and there  are some disturbing lessons the  disturbing lesson is that  this was not a very global moment our  immediate reaction especially 